  // metals-and-non-metals · ncert (book: our Storage copy ncert/chemistry/ch3.pdf = ncert.nic.in jesc103.pdf, Reprint 2026-27) — SOLUTIONS-SCI-2
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-001": { status: "publish", hash: "4aab92f7d0c0cd45", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-002": { status: "hold", note: "third audit (review checklist): Step 3 explains malleability and ductility by metallic bonds and atoms sliding past each other. The chapter never teaches this." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-003": { status: "ref-mismatch", note: "book check MISMATCH (found at In-text Q1 (after §3.2)): The box sits after §3.2.5 (the reactivity series), not §3.2.4. Question text matches." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-SA-001": { status: "ref-mismatch", note: "book check MISMATCH (found at In-text Q2 (after §3.2)): The box follows §3.2.5. Text matches." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-SA-002": { status: "ref-mismatch", note: "book check MISMATCH (found at In-text Q3 (after §3.2)): The book's question depends on a table of metals A–D against FeSO4, CuSO4, ZnSO4 and AgNO3. The row's paraphrase says 'B displaces only Fe' and 'A displaces only Cu'. That is inconsistent with the book. B displaces Fe, so it is more reactive than Cu and should displace Cu. Part (ii) expects displacement when B is added to CuSO4, but the row's data says no reaction. The row also drops the 'No reaction' and 'Displacement' cells for ZnSO4 and AgNO3 for A and B. The ref is also wrong (the box is after §3.2.5)." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-SA-003": { status: "ref-mismatch", note: "book check MISMATCH (found at In-text Q4 (after §3.2)): The box follows §3.2.5. Text matches." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-004": { status: "ref-mismatch", note: "book check MISMATCH (found at In-text Q5 (after §3.2)): The box follows §3.2.5. Text matches." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-LONG-001": { status: "hold", note: "judge/audit: The electron-dot notation '::Ö::' is ambiguous and as written implies about 10 dots instead of 6. The step wording about the dots is also muddled. Rewrite the dot structures clearly." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-005": { status: "publish", hash: "9edc73fcbba82b9c", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-007": { status: "ref-mismatch", note: "book check MISMATCH (found at In-text Q2 (after §3.5)): The box after §3.4 has Q2 'Name two metals found in the free state'. 'Which metals do not corrode easily?' is Q2 of the box after §3.5." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-008": { status: "ref-mismatch", note: "book check MISMATCH (found at In-text Q3 (after §3.5)): The box after §3.4 has Q3 'What chemical process is used for obtaining a metal from its oxide?'. 'What are alloys?' is Q3 of the box after §3.5." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-SA-004": { status: "hold", note: "book question depends on a figure/table the row does not carry: In the book, Q1 is the table of Zn, Mg and Cu against their oxides, followed by 'In which cases will you find displacement reactions taking place?'. The row paraphrases the ask and says 'Fill in the table' but does not carry the table grid. The stem lists the metals and oxides." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-MCQ-001": { status: "publish", hash: "5da7a40e3c25678f", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-MCQ-002": { status: "hold", note: "third audit (review checklist): The key gives (c) zinc coating. The correct NCERT answer is (d) All of the above, because grease, paint and zinc coating all prevent rusting. The steps wrongly reject grease and paint as not durable." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-MCQ-003": { status: "publish", hash: "1fe99ed57d170f0f", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-MCQ-004": { status: "hold", note: "judge/audit: Step 3 says tin is 'below Pb but above H'. In the reactivity series Sn lies above Pb (Fe > Sn > Pb > H). The key is correct but the step is wrong." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-SA-005": { status: "hold", note: "judge/audit: The key puts the conductivity test under (b), which asks for an assessment of the tests. The step claims the malleability test 'fails' for Na and K, but they are soft and deform rather than shatter, so that example is wrong. Better exceptions are mercury (liquid) and brittle zinc." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-009": { status: "publish", hash: "5a7e0626011fe0a7", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-010": { status: "publish", hash: "d678c89a4caeb8c3", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-011": { status: "hold", note: "judge/audit: The step writes M⁺ generically, which wrongly implies a monovalent metal (it fails for Cu²⁺). Use Mⁿ⁺ or a Cu example." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-SA-006": { status: "hold", note: "book question depends on a figure/table the row does not carry: The book refers to 'as shown in figure below' (gas collected by an inverted test tube). The row drops the figure but describes the setup in words, so the question is answerable without it." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-012": { status: "hold", note: "judge/audit: The key lists anodising as a way to prevent rusting of iron. Anodising applies to aluminium, so the key's list of other methods is wrong. Remove it." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-013": { status: "hold", note: "third audit (review checklist): The key and steps name specific acidic and neutral oxides (NO2, P4O10, CO, NO, N2O, H2O) and the H2SO3 and H2CO3 equations. The chapter only says non-metal oxides are acidic or neutral and gives none of these." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-LONG-002": { status: "hold", note: "judge/audit: The equations in (b) are unbalanced (Na + O₂ → Na₂O and Na + H₂O → NaOH + H₂). Part (c) also claims Al does not release harmful Al into food, which is unsupported." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-014": { status: "hold", note: "third audit (review checklist): The key gives the formula CuCO3·Cu(OH)2 and names citric and tartaric acid, none of which is in the chapter. It also describes the tarnish as 'green/black', but the chapter says the basic copper carbonate coat is green." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-SA-007": { status: "publish", hash: "63aaa5635c2036b7", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-SA-008": { status: "hold", note: "third audit (review checklist): Step 2 gives the HAuCl4 equation and the name chloroauric acid, which the chapter never mentions. Step 5 ('Honest cleaning would only polish... Hence the man committed a fraud') is a meta remark with no scientific content. Step 4 largely repeats steps 3 and 4." },
  "METAL-NCERT-3-VSA-015": { status: "hold", note: "third audit (review checklist): Step 3 only restates the conclusion and adds cost, which is outside the chapter. Step 2 says iron's reaction with steam 'forms rust', but that reaction gives Fe3O4 and H2, not rust." },
